  - title: Distance to Plane
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2683.05
      y: -947.09045
      width: 772
      height: 226
    contents:
    - model: {fileID: 8926484042661615017}
      id: 0
      isStickyNote: 0
    - model: {fileID: 8926484042661615021}
      id: 0
      isStickyNote: 0
    - model: {fileID: 8926484042661615042}
      id: 0
      isStickyNote: 0
  stickyNoteInfos:
  - title: 'Sample Mesh Operator:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1070.05
      y: -1060.0905
      width: 493
      height: 837
    contents: "The sample mesh operator allows you to get the position of triangles,
      vertices, and edges of the sampled mesh.\r\n\r\nFirst, we reference the mesh
      we want to sample.\r\nThen we set the placement mode to \u201Csurface,\u201D
      as we want to access the mesh triangles.\r\n\r\nAs we to spawn from all the
      triangles, we're creating a random number between index 0 and the mesh triangle
      count.\r\n\r\nWe're using two different other random numbers so that each particle
      is spawned randomly on the surface of each triangle.\r\n\r\nThe position can
      be used directly to set the particle's position.\r\n\r\nWe're also getting
      the color.x component, which contains ambient occlusion values that have been
      baked into the mesh.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Medium
  - title: 'Distance to Plane:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2681.05
      y: -1050.0905
      width: 336
      height: 100
    contents: We're using a distance-to-plane operator. This operator computes the
      distance value from the plane to each particle's position, creating a gradient
      that will be used to dissolve our particles
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  categories: []
  uiBounds:
    serializedVersion: 2
    x: 1070
    y: -1399
    width: 2878
    height: 2230
